<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>This fictional modern episode addresses the presenting concerns below. [S]</paragraph><list><item>Three months of looser, more frequent stools with dark blood mixed in on several days, tiredness, and about 6 kg of unintended weight loss. [S]</item><item>Wants plain information about what is wrong and wants to keep working on his books through treatment. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>David Hume was born at Edinburgh on April 26, 1711, Old Style (May 7, 1711, in the Gregorian calendar), the second son of Joseph Hume of Ninewells in Berwickshire. [D]</item><item>In My Own Life Hume wrote that his father died when he was an infant, leaving him, an elder brother, and a sister in their mother&apos;s care, and that his mother had died by the time he went to live at his brother&apos;s country house in 1749. [D]</item><item>In a letter to a physician written about 1734, Hume described an illness beginning about September 1729, when he was eighteen: loss of his ardour for study, scurvy spots on his fingers, and from about April 1730 a watery mouth, which his physician called &apos;the disease of the learned&apos;; he was treated with bitters, anti-hysteric pills, a pint of claret a day, and riding, and in 1731 had a ravenous appetite and palpitations. [D]</item><item>In My Own Life, dated April 18, 1776, Hume wrote that in spring 1775 he was struck with a disorder in his bowels, which at first gave him no alarm but which he came to judge mortal and incurable; he wrote that he had suffered very little pain and that his spirits had never abated. [D]</item></list><paragraph>6 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.20" extension="2015-08-01"/>
    <code code="11348-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Past illness note"/>
    <title>Past Medical History</title>
    <text><paragraph>Historical biography with true dates. These events are not part of the synthetic care timeline.</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Historical date</th><th>History</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>September 1729</td><td>Illness from about the age of eighteen, given the contemporary label &apos;the disease of the learned&apos;, with scurvy spots on the fingers, a watery mouth, and later a ravenous appetite and palpitations; treated with bitters, anti-hysteric pills, claret, and daily riding</td><td>D (CLM-0003)</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

