<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Colonoscopy with biopsy, CT of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis, and baseline CEA for iron deficiency anemia with rectal bleeding in a man of 64. Guideline: NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Colon Cancer, Version 4.2025 (2025); AGA Clinical Practice Guidelines on the Gastrointestinal Evaluation of Iron Deficiency Anemia (2020); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, colorectal cancer and anemia monographs (2026) [S]</item><item>Medical oncology consultation to review staging, the indeterminate liver lesion, the order of treatment, and adjuvant therapy. Guideline: NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Colon Cancer, Version 4.2025 (2025) [S]</item><item>Intravenous ferric carboxymaltose before surgery to correct iron deficiency anemia, with ferritin and blood count rechecked six to eight weeks later. Guideline: AGA Clinical Practice Guidelines on the Gastrointestinal Evaluation of Iron Deficiency Anemia (2020); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, colorectal cancer and anemia monographs (2026) [S]</item><item>Laparoscopic sigmoid colectomy with lymph node dissection by the colorectal surgery team. Guideline: NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Colon Cancer, Version 4.2025 (2025) [S]</item><item>Three months (four cycles) of adjuvant CAPOX for low-risk stage III disease, after DPYD genotyping, with neuropathy, blood count, and diarrhea checks at every visit. Guideline: NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Colon Cancer, Version 4.2025 (2025); Duration of Adjuvant Chemotherapy for Stage III Colon Cancer (IDEA collaboration). N Engl J Med 2018;378:1177-1188 (2018); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, colorectal cancer and anemia monographs (2026) [S]</item><item>Survivorship surveillance: history, examination, and CEA every 3 to 6 months for 2 years, then every 6 months to 5 years; CT of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis every 6 to 12 months for 5 years; colonoscopy one year after resection. Guideline: The American Society of Colon and Rectal Surgeons Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Surveillance and Survivorship Care of Patients After Curative Treatment of Colon and Rectal Cancer (2021); American Cancer Society Colorectal Cancer Survivorship Care Guidelines (2015); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, colorectal cancer and anemia monographs (2026) [S]</item><item>Distress and depression screening at each visit, at least 150 minutes of activity a week, alcohol at or below one drink a day, and a written survivorship care plan. Guideline: American Cancer Society Colorectal Cancer Survivorship Care Guidelines (2015); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, colorectal cancer and anemia monographs (2026) [S]</item><item ID="referral-order">Referral to medical oncology, Rohan Mehta, MD, ordered 2026-05-11 [S]</item></list></text>

